          %p
          %h2#section_Idea Idea
          %p
            %em Category theory
            is a toolset for describing the general abstract structures in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
          %h3#paradigm Paradigm
          %p
            As opposed to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,y, \cdots
            – called
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            %strong relations
            between these objects: the (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"}> homo
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"}> morphism
            s between them
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi f
                  %mi y
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  x \stackrel{f}{\to} y
                  \\,.
          %p
            Later this will lead naturally on to an infinite sequence of steps: first
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category+theory"} 2-category theory
            which focuses on relation between relations, morphisms between morphisms:
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-morphism"} 2-morphism
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategories"} bicategories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gray+categories"} Gray categories
            …. Eventually this leads to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            = succeed "-morphisms" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p =–
          %p
            The concept that formalizes this is that of a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composed
            if they are adjacent
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi x
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mi f
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi y
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mi g
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi z
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ↦
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi x
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mi g
                                  %mo ∘
                                  %mi f
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi z
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\left(
                  \\array{
                  x
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{f}}
                  \\\
                  y
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{g}}
                  \\\
                  z
                  }
                  \\;\right)
                  \\;\;\;\;
                  \\mapsto
                  \\;\;\;\;
                  
                  \\left(
                  \\array{
                  x
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{g \circ f}}
                  \\\
                  z
                  }
                  \\;\;\;\right)
                  \\,.
          %h3#examples Examples
          %p
            The archetypical example of a category is the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
          %p
            The classical examples of categories are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+category"} concrete categories
            whose
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff%2C+structure%2C+property"} sets with extra structure
            and whose
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} Grp
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
            %em category
            derives: these categories literally
            %em categorize
            mathematical structures by packing structures of the same
            %em type
            (same category) and structure preserving mappings between them into a single whole structure, a category.
          %p
            But it is far from the case that all categories are of this type. Categories are much more versatile than these classical examples suggest. After all, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            is just a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver"} quiver
            (a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+graph"} directed graph
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            (in a sense called
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horizontal+categorification"} horizontal categorification
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjunctions
          %p
            Archetypical examples of non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+category"} concrete
            categories are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+category"} fundamental category
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+space"} directed space
          %h3#terminology Terminology
          %p
            Categories were named after the examples of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+category"} concrete categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+Mac+Lane"} Saunders Mac Lane
            writes
          %blockquote
            %p Now the discovery of ideas as general as these is chiefly the willingness to make a brash or speculative abstraction, in this case supported by the pleasure of purloining words from the philosophers: “Category” from Aristotle and Kant, “Functor” from Carnap (Logische Syntax der Sprache), and “natural transformation” from then current informal parlance.
            %p
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+Mac+Lane"}> Saunders Mac Lane
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories+for+the+Working+Mathematician"} Categories for the Working Mathematician
          %p
            However, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} categories
            of category theory are way more general than these
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+category"} concrete categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+%28philosophy%29"} term
            in philosophy is not particularly related to what Eilenberg &amp; Mac Lane did with it.
          %h3#the_basic_trinity_of_concepts The basic trinity of concepts
          %p
            Category theory reflects on itself. Categories are about collections of morphisms. And there are evident morphisms between categories:
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
          %p This trinity of concepts
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
          %p
            is what category theory is built on. In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            this continues with
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              \-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transfor"}> transfor
              s for all
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in \mathbb{N}
          %p
            But these are just the bookkeeping tools. The actual
            %em theory
            of categories is in the concept of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunctions"} adjunctions
            (defined, in turn, in terms of
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} duality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+constructions"} universal constructions
            that are at the heart of category theory – (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"}> co
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"}> limits
            , (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coends"}> co
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ends"} ends
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+extensions"} Kan extensions
          %h3#conceptual_unification Conceptual unification
          %p
            A major driving force behind the development of category theory is its ability to abstract and unify concepts. General statements about categories apply to each specific
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+category"} concrete category
            of mathematical structures. The general notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+constructions"} universal constructions
            in categories, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functors"} representable functors
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjoint functors
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
          %h3#AbstractNonsense “General abstract nonsense”
          %p
            This abstraction power of category theory has led
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Norman+Steenrod"} Norman Steenrod
            to coin the term
            %em abstract nonsense
            or
            %em general abstract nonsense
            for it. It is being used as in “This property is not specific to this context, it already follows from general abstract nonsense”.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Freyd"} Peter Freyd
            expressed a similar feeling by his witticism:
          %blockquote
            %p#WhatIsTriviallyTrivial “Perhaps the purpose of categorical algebra is to show that which is trivial is trivially trivial.”
          %p
            But abstract nonsense still tends to meet with some resistance. In the preface of
            %a{:href => "#Mitchell65"} Mitchell (1965)
            it says:
          %blockquote
            %p A number of sophisticated people tend to disparage category theory as consistently as others disparage certain kinds of classical music. When obliged to speak of a category they do so in an apologetic tone, similar to the way some say, “It was a gift – I’ve never even played it” when a record of Chopin Nocturnes is discovered in their possession. For this reason I add to the usual prerequisite that the reader have a fair amount of mathematical sophistication, the further prerequisite that he have no other kind.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"} Alexander Grothendieck
            (in a letter to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronald+Brown"} Ronald Brown
            on 5th May 1982,
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "https://csg.igrothendieck.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/U24d.pdf#page=11"} here
          %blockquote
            %p The introduction of the cipher 0 or the group concept was general nonsense too, and mathematics was more or less stagnating for thousands of years because nobody was around to take such childish steps…
          %h3#the_npov The nPOV
          %p
            The vast applicability and expressiveness of category theory leads to the observation that most structures in mathematics are best understood from a category theoretic or higher category theoretic viewpoint. This is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nPOV"} nPOV
          %p
          %h2#section_The_central_constructions The central constructions
          %p
          %h3#section_Presheaves Presheaves
          %p
            Much of the power of category theory rests in the fact that it reflects on itself. For instance that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            between two categories form themselves a category: the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor category
          %p
            This leads to the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf+categories"} presheaf categories
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+toposes"} sheaf toposes
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
          %p
            Under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isbell+duality"} Isbell duality
            this sets the stage for everything in mathematics related to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            and their duality.
          %p
          %h3#section_Universal_constructions Universal constructions
          %p
            Elementary as it is, the definition of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            supports a powerful set of constructions:
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+constructions"} universal constructions
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
                and
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"}> adjunction
                ;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+extension"} Kan extension
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/end"} end
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
          %p All these are special cases of each other and thus reflect different aspect of one single phenomenon. Applying category theory means applying these constructions in specific situations and using general abstract theorems for deducing statements about concrete contexts.
          %p
          %h3#section_The_central_theorems The central theorems
          %p Category theory has a handful of central lemmas and theorems. Their proof is typically easy, sometimes almost tautological. Their power rests in the fact that they apply over and over again all over mathematics. Many concrete constructions get simplified by observing that they are but special realizations of these general abstract results in category theory. Among these central theorems are
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                first and foremost: the
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isbell+duality"}> Isbell duality
                ;
            %li
              %p
                the
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+construction"} Grothendieck construction
            %li
              %p
                the
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor+theorem"} adjoint functor theorem
            %li
              %p
                the
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadicity+theorem"} monadicity theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannaka+duality"}> Tannaka duality
                \.
          %p
          %h2#section_Applications Applications
          %p For a detailed list of applications see
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/applications+of+%28higher%29+category+theory"}> applications of (higher) category theory
              \.
          %p
          %h3#section_In_pure_mathematics In pure mathematics
          %p Apart from its general role in mathematics, category theory provides the high-level language for
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
                \/
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+algebra"} higher algebra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
                \.
          %p
          %h3#section_Outside_of_mathematics Outside of mathematics
          %p Outside of pure mathematics, category theory finds major applications in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                fundamental
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
                – see
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory+and+physics"} higher category theory and physics
            %li
              %p
                theoretical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computer+science"} computer science
          %p
            and is increasingly finding applications in such diverse areas as chemistry, network theory and natural language processing. Further information can be found on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/applied+category+theory"} applied category theory
            page.
          %p
          %h2#section_Contrast_to_theories_of_other_objects Contrast to theories of other objects
          %p
          %h3{:id => "section_Category_theory_vs._set_theory"} Category theory vs. set theory
          %p
            Here set theory is assumed to be a theory of the usual concept of sets, that is
            %em material
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
          %p
            No one of these is more fundamental than the other as a foundation of mathematics. Category theory is a holistic (structural) approach to mathematics that can (through such methods as Lawvere’s
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            of mathematics and (through
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+set+theory"} algebraic set theory
            = succeed "es" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class"} class
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inaccessible+cardinal"} inaccessible cardinal
            exists or even that
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+universe"} Grothendieck universe
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th Set theory
                %th
                %th Category theory
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} membership relation
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} -
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} sets
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} categories
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} elements
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} objects
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} -
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} morphisms
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} functions
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} functors
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} equations between elements
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} isomorphisms between objects
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} equations between sets
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} equivalences between categories
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} equations between functions
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} natural transformations between functors
          %p Lawvere pointed out that set theory is axiomatized by a binary membership relation while category theory is axiomatized by a ternary composition relation.
          %p
            The process of going from sets to categories is a special case of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
            and the reverse process is a special case of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decategorification"} decategorification
          %p For a philosophical consideration of foundations covering and comparing sets, structuralism (a la Bourbaki?) and categories, see the article
          %ul
            %li Sets, categories and structuralism - Costas Drossos
          %p
          %h3{:id => "section_Category_theory_vs._order_theory"} Category theory vs. order theory
          %p
            A category may be thought of as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            rather than of a
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order+theory"} order theory
          %p
            See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory+vs+order+theory"} category theory vs order theory
            for more discussion.
